# Model weights — never commit. A single bge-small Q8_0 GGUF is ~130MB and
# would bloat git history permanently.
*.gguf
# Everything under models/ is ignored so no weight can ever be staged by
# accident. Written as `models/*` rather than `models/` because git refuses to
# re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, which would make the
# negation below impossible. The net effect is identical: weights are blocked,
# the directory itself is tracked so it exists on a fresh clone.
models/*
!models/.gitkeep

# Vector store artifacts
vectors/
*.npy
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# Environment / secrets
.env

# Editor / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# ── Terraform ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.terraform/` is the local plugin/cache directory. On this repo it holds the
# compiled hashicorp/aws provider binary at roughly 600MB. It is rebuilt by
# every `terraform init` and is never committed — no value to a future clone,
# enormous permanent cost in git history if it lands.
.terraform/

# Terraform state. Ignored even though `infra/bootstrap`'s state currently holds
# nothing sensitive beyond the bucket name, for three reasons: state is machine
# state rather than source code and is not meaningfully reviewable in a diff;
# it is environment-specific and must never be carried between environments;
# and the main stack at `infra/` will reference the RDS endpoint and the secret
# ARN. That no plaintext password is expected in that state is a direct payoff
# of `manage_master_user_password`, but one database's absence of plaintext is
# not a reason to start tracking state.
*.tfstate
# State backups Terraform writes alongside the live file, e.g.
# terraform.tfstate.backup — these are full copies of the same content.
*.tfstate.*

# Terraform crash logs. These can contain the values of variables as they were
# at the time of the crash, so they are treated as possible secret spill.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# `.terraform.lock.hcl` is DELIBERATELY not ignored — this asymmetry looks like
# an oversight and is not. The lock file pins provider versions and
# checksums, so a future `terraform init` cannot silently resolve a different
# AWS provider than the one that last ran. Ignoring it would let plan/apply
# behaviour change underneath the operator with no diff anywhere to show for it.
# The copy under `infra/bootstrap/` is committed alongside this rule for the
# same reason.